\f1\fs22 Chip Stockwell stood in the middle of his new townhouse, surrounded by boxes he hadn\'92t yet unpacked. The place was immaculate \'97 a spacious three-bedroom two-storey townhouse with minimalist decor and high-end finishes that made his previous Fridge Force quarters look like a server rack with a shower. Through floor-to-ceiling windows, Sublime Point\'92s manicured residential circuit looked like the kind of place where people jogged voluntarily.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Home sweet home,\'94 he muttered to no one in the kitchen, running his fingers along the quartz countertop. The countertop, he noted, was made of the kind of stone that rich people used to demonstrate they could afford to flatten mountains for aesthetic purposes. He filed this information away. In Chip\'92s experience, employers who gave you things this nice were employers who wanted you to remember what you stood to lose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tried to avert his gaze from the door to the stasis closet. He\'92d never lived with one before, always preferring an old-fashioned chilling fridge. Maybe he could get this one decommissioned and turned into\'85 
\f2\i I dunno, a wine cellar
\f1\i0 , he thought. He shook his head; he\'92d never been interested in wine, never finding anything that was compatible with his fast food diet. Mostly because of the glasses, he supposed. He ate most meals straight from the paper bags and wrappers, and had no use for cutlery, so how the tax would he handle a wine glass? He was sure he would continually tip it over or splinter its fine stem in his bear-sized hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>How would he order fast food now? Would anyone deliver to Eloise\'92s compound? And if they did, what would the delivery driver have to sign, surrender, or submit to? He\'92d counted five security checkpoints between the main gate and his front door. Five was a lot. Five suggested an employer who didn\'92t trust her own employees, which, in Chip\'92s experience, meant an employer worth working for.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Within hours of unpacking his essentials, he\'92d found the screen he\'92d used to divide his one-room living area into dining and TV spaces. His new home had separate TV, dining, and living rooms, so he leant the partially folded screen against the stasis closet door. Out of sight, but never out of mind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Project Kebab administrative offices occupied the lowest sub-level of the building that was the farthest away from everything: the residential circuit, the main house, the administration buildings and even the spectacular views over Jamison Valley. Chip descended in an elevator that required both biometric verification and a keycard. The elevator\'92s interior panel listed twelve sub-levels. Only one button \'97 the lowest \'97 responded to his clearance. He noted this. Limited access meant limited escape routes, but it also meant limited witnesses.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The doors opened onto a modest lounge area, the seating furniture of which Chip could see would lack the sturdiness for his comfort. There was a lonely water cooler against the far wall and Chip wondered what vintage the water in its large inverted blue bottle would be. There was no evidence of disposable cups. Two exits: the elevator behind him and the lab doors ahead. No stairs. No fire escape visible. In the event of an emergency, Chip\'92s options would be limited to prayer and cushioning.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To the left was a pair of doors marked simply \'93K-LAB.\'94 As Chip approached, the doors slid open. Beyond, he saw an expansive laboratory space with workstations arranged in concentric circles around a central raised glassed-in platform. Thankfully, no sign of a Gyges Disc anywhere, though the platform itself had the look of something built to contain things that didn\'92t want to be contained. Chip recognised the type. Fridge Force had similar platforms, only less well-funded and more stained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The famous Chip Stockwell,\'94 came a voice from his left. \'93I\'92ve heard so little about you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A bald man in an immaculate suit approached, hand extended. He was ruddy-skinned and soft-bodied, pear-shaped in the way of men who spent their lives sitting down and taking credit for the work of people who stood up. His smile didn\'92t reach his eyes, which were watery and pale.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You must be The Johnny,\'94 Chip replied, using the moniker Sarah had briefed him on, and accepted the handshake with his own clammy baseball mitt-sized claw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just Johnny will do,\'94 the man said, grip firm and deliberate. \'93Or Mr Stoat. Unless you\'92re one of my life-documenters, in which case I\'92m your worst nightmare.\'94 He laughed, as if this was a joke they were both in on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m just a stasis anomalies investigator,\'94 Chip grumbled, and he did sound something like a bear. He noted the positions of the workstations, the CCTV cameras, the fire suppression system. Three staff at consoles. None of them had looked up. Either they were very busy or very accustomed to ignoring Johnny, and Chip suspected the latter.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There\'92s no \'91just\'92 anything at Project Kebab, Stockwell. Everyone here has a purpose. Some of us know what that purpose is.\'94 Johnny stood at an angle, opening up the passage between desks. \'93Come into my office,\'94 he invited. \'93Unless you would like to see the Kebabs first.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The what?\'94 Chip wondered if The Johnny knew how to smuggle Lebanese take-away into the facility.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know,\'94 Johnny whispered. \'93The Gy\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, tax no,\'94 Chip blurted, then covered with, \'93Plenty of time for that later.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In his office, Johnny offered Chip a seat and launched into what was clearly a rehearsed monologue about the revolutionary potential of Gyges Disc technology, the myopia of Fridge Force\'92s applications, and the word \'93nuclear,\'94 which he seemed to find exciting rather than cautionary. Through the glass wall, Chip could see the lab staff still not looking up. He wondered whether Johnny\'92s office had been soundproofed for their benefit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I mean, these Kebabs could be the greatest thing since nuclear,\'94 Johnny concluded, with the satisfaction of a man who had never personally been near anything that had gone nuclear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip gagged. \'93Excuse me,\'94 he spluttered. \'93I think I ate some bad\'85\'94 He hefted his great weight off the chair and lumbered out of the room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From the hall, Chip heard Johnny mutter something about \'93field operatives\'94 to no one in particular. Good. The man was already writing him off. Chip had spent fifteen promotions perfecting the art of being underestimated, and Johnny was the kind of superior who made it easy \'97 so busy broadcasting his own competence that he never checked anyone else\'92s frequencies. Chip filed him under manageable and went to find Sarah.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah,\'94 Johnny announced, materialising behind him with proprietary satisfaction, \'93your new pet has arrived.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked up from a console in a smaller office off the main laboratory. She fixed Chip with the kind of gaze that preceded either a job offer or a sentencing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ignore Johnny, Mr Stockwell. I\'92m Sarah Jacobson.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip grunted. Sarah gestured him to a chair. As he took his seat, Chip noticed three elderly individuals sitting silently in the corner of the room. Their presence was so unobtrusive that he\'92d completely missed them upon entering. He didn\'92t like missing things. He especially didn\'92t like missing three entire people.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Sages,\'94 Sarah said, following his gaze.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The three nodded in unison, but didn\'92t speak. Chip noted their positions relative to the door. They were sitting where people sat when they wanted to observe without being observed, which was precisely where Chip would have sat if he\'92d got there first.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now,\'94 Sarah said, pulling up a holographic display of a Gyges Disc. \'93How much do you know about how stasis technology actually works?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I know enough to know you shouldn\'92t be asking me that in front of\'97\'94 he gestured vaguely at the room \'93\'97anyone.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Humour me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip folded his arms, which was not easy given the size of both the arms and the chair. \'93Stasis units preserve food. They slow biological decay. That\'92s the official line and I\'92m comfortable with it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you, though?\'94 Sarah asked. \'93Because I\'92ve read your file, and you\'92ve spent twenty years monitoring anomalies in technology that supposedly just keeps milk from going sour. Twenty years, fifteen declined promotions, and a membership to the Global Stasis Technicians Association that makes you essentially unsackable. That\'92s a lot of effort for someone who\'92s comfortable.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip said nothing. Most people found silence uncomfortable and filled it with useful information. Sarah appeared to be one of the people who found silence comfortable and used it as a weapon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you building a weapon here?\'94 Chip asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s expression shifted. Not much. \'93No,\'94 she said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That wasn\'92t a comfortable no.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It was a complicated no. We\'92re researching applications that other people could weaponise if we don\'92t understand them first. The discs create localised chronological variances \'97 micro-pockets of altered time. That\'92s how stasis works. Not temperature. Time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I know how stasis works,\'94 Chip said quietly, and for the first time since arriving, he wasn\'92t grumbling.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo sat forward between the other Sages and said, \'93I\'92m twenty-six.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip regarded the wizened old husk of a man and said, \'93That\'92s nice. I hope you lived a full life.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Twenty-six. Hundred. Years. Old,\'94 Solo schooled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip blinked away the absurdity of Solo\'92s claim, and cast his eye over the others in the room. He said to Johnny, \'93I don\'92t care what Goldilocks and the three fossils think. It\'92s impossible.\'94 He heaved his weight off the chair. \'93This was a mistake. I\'92m leaving.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You can\'92t leave,\'94 Sarah stated flatly. \'93You know this is too important to leave in anyone else\'92s hands. We need you. We need you to assess the risk of our research and tell us when we\'92re going too far.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Still standing and trying to ignore the complaints of his feet, Chip answered, \'93That\'92s easy. You\'92re already going too far.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That cannot be true, young Chip,\'94 Kleoboulos stated gently. \'93Your own Fridge Force has been harnessing and monitoring these instruments for many a long year.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And we\'92re all terrified of when something catastrophic will happen with them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which is why we need you,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip looked at her. She looked back. It occurred to him that she was not lying, which was unusual in an employer and therefore suspicious. He also noticed that the holographic display was still rotating behind her, and despite every instinct that told him to walk out and keep walking, his eyes were already on the data cascading alongside the disc\'92s image. The readings were wrong. Not wrong in a way that meant the equipment was faulty \'97 wrong in a way that meant everything Fridge Force had ever told him was incomplete. He\'92d suspected this for years. He\'92d just never expected someone to show him the proof and ask for his opinion on it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Fine,\'94 he said, and sat back down, which was harder than standing up and involved a sound from the chair that suggested it, too, had opinions about his participation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time Saturday arrived, Chip had settled into a routine of sorts. Mornings and afternoons were spent at the lab, evenings were devoted to unpacking and setting up his townhouse, and nights were plagued by dreams of Fridge Force agents breaking down his door. The most frightening part of those nightmares was that none of the arresting officers seemed to know his name.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He\'92d installed three additional locks and a private security system that wasn\'92t connected to Sublime Point\'92s network which, unbeknown to him, Eloise had already neutralised. She also knew that the generous signing bonus she\'92d given Chip had already been split between multiple anonymous accounts, ready for Chip\'92s quick escape if necessary. This was, to Eloise, confirmation that she\'92d hired the right man. Anyone who trusted their new employer on the first week was either naive or stupid, and Chip was conspicuously neither.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Contrary to his fears, this job stressed him no more than his Fridge Force gig. While expanding stasis tech beyond its currently mild applications was precisely the thing he\'92d spent his career preventing, he realised he\'92d always lived with the expectation that sooner or later, it would blow up in his face. Now that he had a clearer idea of how that might actually happen, he was somehow more at peace with it. He supposed this was like the difference between fearing spiders and working at a spider farm. The spiders were still there, but at least someone was paying you to watch them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Still, if there was one thing he was more stressed about, it was the family living in the townhouse next door. The Chuglinghams, the family he had not long ago been instrumental in sending to offshore detention. He felt no guilt for it, only mounting frustration at how happy they were. While they had been avoiding him assiduously, it was evident by the sounds of activities that came from their yard and through their open windows, they were getting on with their lives while he, essentially, didn\'92t have one. They were happy and chirpy and it annoyed the tax out of him. The kids were worse. They were chirpy and loud. Chip had no idea that laughter could sound so threatening.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While he breakfasted on a burrito (Eloise had personally hired a Fast Food Delivery Consultant for him \'97 a title Chip suspected had been invented specifically for this purpose), Chip checked his security system (which he still believed was functioning) on his encrypted tablet (which Eloise had easily jailbroken). On the dark web portal he\'92d set up for monitoring Fridge Force communications he found what he feared \'97 an internal bulletin listing him as a \'93person of interest\'94 in an investigation of technological espionage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip stared at the bulletin for a long moment. He walked to the kitchen and faced the stasis unit he\'92d been avoiding. With a deep breath, he removed the decorative screen and opened the unit\'92s maintenance panel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He\'92d spent twenty years guarding this technology from the outside. If Fridge Force was coming for him, it was time to learn it from the inside.
